<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Arab Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as UAR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The United Arab Republic was a sovereign state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> from 1958 to 1971, formed as a political union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a>. It was established on 1 February 1958 at the urging of Syrian leaders, as a first step towards a larger pan-Arab state, and referendums on 21 February approved the merger and Nasser as president. Nasser demanded the dismantling of Syria's political parties, squeezed Syrians out of positions of influence and in 1961 nationalized banks and heavy industry; the backlash led to the Syrian coup of 28 September 1961 and Syria's secession. Egypt kept the name until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a> formally dissolved it in September 1971.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1958 an insurrection by Muslims who wanted Lebanon to join the <b>United Arab Republic</b> brought 5,000 United States Marines to Beirut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Nasser it led the Arab world: Sudan gained independence from the Anglo-Egyptian Condominium on 1 January 1956, Egypt joined Syria in the <b>United Arab Republic</b> from 1958 until Syria's secession in 1961, and the Voice of the Arabs radio called for unity and revolution across the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Independence brought coups by Husni al-Za'im, Sami al-Hinnawi and Adib Shishakli in 1949, a return to parliament in 1954, a pact with the Soviet Union in 1956 and, on 1 February 1958, union with Egypt as the <b>United Arab Republic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The UAE is the most successful federation formed among Arab states in the twentieth century, surviving where projects of Arab union from the <b>United Arab Republic</b> onward failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Egypt kept the name “the <b>United Arab Republic</b>” after Syria left the union in 1961, implying that it remained open to union with other Arab countries, and renamed itself the Arab Republic of Egypt only in 1973.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained the anthem until 1979, and served too as the anthem of the <b>United Arab Republic</b> and, from 1963 to 1981, of Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li></ul></section>
